# data intensive applications 
large quantities of data or the complexity of data or the speed at which it is changing 

# CPU intensive applications 
CPU cycles are the bottlenecks 

Reliability, scalability and maintenability
### Reliability - tolerating hardware and software faults, human error
### Scalability - measuring load and performance… latency percentives and throughput
### Maintainability - operability, simplicity and evolvability


## Standard building blocks of data intensive applications:
Store data so that they, or another application, can find it again later (databases)
• Remember the result of an expensive operation, to speed up reads (caches)
• Allow users to search data by keyword or filter it in various ways (search indexes)
• Send a message to another process, to be handled asynchronously (stream pro‐
cessing)
• Periodically crunch a large amount of accumulated data (batch processing)

## data systems:
databases, message queues, caches... databases and message queues are similar such that they both store data for sometime but their access patterns are different.
- there are datastores that are also used as message queues (Redis), 
- and there are message queues with database-like durability guarantees (Apache Kafka).

memcache - application managed caching layer
elasticsearch - a full text search server
it is the application's code responsibility to keep these cashes and indexes in sync with the main database